 You're hired.

155
00:14:56,580 --> 00:14:57,920
 You'll work with Adams here.

156
00:14:59,630 --> 00:15:01,420
 It's me, Adams.

157
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:04,670
 You coming?

158
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:06,230
 It's time we were at work.

159
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,570
 Hey, what are you doing with all that, huh?

160
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,170
 Well, I'm really interested in doing experiments.

161
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:14,550
 Electricity, chemistry, everything, you understand?

162
00:15:14,670 --> 00:15:18,830
 Right now, I'm doing tests on experiments carried out by a Swedish chemist, Alfred Nobel.

163
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:20,190
 But I think I'll stop.

164
00:15:20,690 --> 00:15:22,190
 Could be a little dangerous.

165
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:26,580
 What exactly is it?

166
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:30,710
 Oh, it's a mixture of nitroglycerin and a paste with other things.

167
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:32,190
 Nobel calls it dynamite.

168
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:33,330
 What's this?

169
00:15:33,650 --> 00:15:34,360
 A multiplex.

170
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:36,560
 Oh, what does it do?

171
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:39,950
 Well, you can transmit several messages over the same line.

172
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:41,200
 Watch it, Adams.

173
00:15:41,670 --> 00:15:42,560
 Don't touch it.

174
00:15:43,250 --> 00:15:45,250
 Pull the wires. You'll be electrocuted.

175
00:15:46,150 --> 00:15:48,630
 It's acid.

176
00:15:49,330 --> 00:15:49,950
 Wash it off.

177
00:15:49,950 --> 00:15:53,580
 You're not a pretty sight. You should be more careful.

178
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:56,860
 That? It's a machine for counting ballots.

179
00:15:56,860 --> 00:16:01,050
 I'm going to present it to Congress in Washington and I'll get rich.

180
00:16:01,050 --> 00:16:02,860
 Hmm...

181
00:16:04,190 --> 00:16:07,060
 I'll explain, Senator.

182
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:09,380
 This machine automatically counts votes.

183
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:11,460
 You'll gain an enormous amount of time.

184
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:17,770
 We're going to save time, my boy.

185
00:16:17,770 --> 00:16:20,250
 You ought to know we don't want to save time.

186
00:16:20,250 --> 00:16:22,330
 We want to save our seats in Congress.

187
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:26,310
 Let me just explain it. It's really simple.

188
00:16:26,310 --> 00:16:29,410
 Then you can decide for yourself whether or not my invention is useful.

189
00:16:29,410 --> 00:16:46,590
 A few more unsuccessful attempts.

190
00:16:47,130 --> 00:16:49,970
 And at 22, he was up to his ears in debts.

191
00:16:50,330 --> 00:16:51,730
 Edison had lost everything.

192
00:16:51,830 --> 00:16:52,990
 The Civil War was over.

193
00:16:53,510 --> 00:16:56,670
 And for the United States, the period was extraordinary.

194
00:16:57,230 --> 00:17:05,080
 And New York became a financial center with its first skyscraper and a steam-driven elevator.

195
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:08,360
 The railroad links the Atlantic and the Pacific.

196
00:17:08,740 --> 00:17:12,400
 Baseball becomes a national sport, and roller skating was very fashionable.

197
00:17:15,220 --> 00:17:17,780
 One dollar, the last one.

198
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:19,460
 Well, it won't take me very far.

199
00:17:19,460 --> 00:17:22,940
 Not in coffee.

200
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:23,640
 How much?

201
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:40,460
 That'll be 10 cents for you, son, the house.

202
00:17:41,610 --> 00:17:44,390
 The machine doesn't work. It's useless.

203
00:17:44,650 --> 00:17:45,530
 That's a piece of junk.

204
00:17:45,910 --> 00:17:47,400
 But it's your fault, Lars.

205
00:17:47,690 --> 00:17:49,610
 You're a nincompoop, Pope.

206
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:51,720
 If we can't send messages, I'm ruined.

207
00:17:52,030 --> 00:17:53,330
 Pardon me, gentlemen.

208
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:53,590
 What?

209
00:17:54,090 --> 00:17:57,920
 I'm pretty sure that I can repair your broken machine.

210
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:02,750
 You understand them?

211
00:18:02,910 --> 00:18:03,770
 Yes, in a way.

212
00:18:04,050 --> 00:18:04,680
 Come on.

213
00:18:09,830 --> 00:18:14,030
 Yes, I can repair it.

214
00:18:15,190 --> 00:18:17,020
 Leave me alone for a moment.

215
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:19,910
 It's all right. The young man inspires confidence.

216
00:18:22,010 --> 00:18:32,720
 Machine is working now.

217
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:38,780
 You're hired, young man. $300 per month.

218
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:39,540
 Will that do?

219
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:40,640
 Oh, yes.

220
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:41,880
 Well, it's a beginning.

221
00:18:42,470 --> 00:18:44,050
 Mr. Edison, I'll buy your machine.

222
00:18:44,850 --> 00:18:45,980
 Five thousand dollars.

223
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:47,000
 All right.

224
00:18:47,140 --> 00:18:47,620
 All right.

225
00:18:48,030 --> 00:18:51,790
 Mr. Edison, Western Union is interested in your synchronization system.

226
00:18:52,390 --> 00:18:54,090
 How much?

227
00:18:54,570 --> 00:18:56,580
 I'm waiting to hear your offer.

228
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,790
 I would like four thousand.

229
00:18:59,140 --> 00:19:00,370
 I would take three.

230
00:19:00,610 --> 00:19:03,910
 Let's say, um, would you take forty thousand?

231
00:19:04,350 --> 00:19:05,290
 Yes, I would.

232
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:12,300
 Mr. Edison will buy your patent for the multiplex telegraph.

233
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:13,400
 What is your price?

234
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:15,300
 $25,000, Mr. Orton.

235
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:17,250
 Deal.

236
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:19,650
 Plus the annual fees, of course.

237
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:22,960
 Of course, Mr. Edison.

238
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:25,920
 I'll take the entire floor.

239
00:19:27,370 --> 00:19:30,690
 The fact is I need offices as well as workshops and a production area.

240
00:19:31,050 --> 00:19:31,370
 Hmm.

241
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:46,320
 Scared me.

242
00:19:46,560 --> 00:19:48,340
 I scared you? You mean it? I really did?

243
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:50,400
 Not really.

244
00:19:51,550 --> 00:19:53,850
 I hope not, because I want to marry you.

245
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:56,250
 Listen.

246
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:09,960
 What are you doing here today?

247
00:20:10,130 --> 00:20:12,140
 You can see I'm trying to repair this machine.

248
00:20:12,590 --> 00:20:13,830
 Gotten what day it is?

249
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:14,820
 What?

250
00:20:15,100 --> 00:20:16,470
 For a start, it's Christmas.

251
00:20:17,110 --> 00:20:19,300
 What's more, you just got married.

252
00:20:19,470 --> 00:20:20,150
 Good Lord.

253
00:20:20,150 --> 00:20:27,600
 But we simply must pay our taxes or we'll have a terrible fine as well.

254
00:20:27,770 --> 00:20:29,240
 You're right. I'll pay him, yes.

255
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:31,630
 Please, go now.

256
00:20:33,190 --> 00:20:33,580
 Huh?

257
00:20:43,810 --> 00:20:54,560
 A daydream, are you?

258
00:20:55,910 --> 00:20:58,650
 My name is, uh...

259
00:20:58,650 --> 00:20:59,710
 Oh, never mind.

260
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:00,320
 Next.

261
00:21:00,770 --> 00:21:07,760
 And before long, he had set up what is the very first research and development laboratory anywhere in Menlo Park, New Jersey.

262
