 When I turn it, the needle cuts a continuous groove.

268
00:21:29,310 --> 00:21:30,330
 My voice is recorded.

269
00:21:30,750 --> 00:21:31,530
 Now, listen carefully.

270
00:21:31,670 --> 00:21:32,170
 I'll turn it.

271
00:21:35,420 --> 00:21:38,340
 Yes, I know.

272
00:21:38,620 --> 00:21:39,940
 It still requires a lot of work.

273
00:21:40,420 --> 00:21:42,520
 Meanwhile, I'm working on another project.

274
00:21:43,870 --> 00:21:45,550
 Now, this thread is carbonized.

275
00:21:46,270 --> 00:21:48,650
 It can be heated by electricity and not burn.

276
00:21:49,090 --> 00:21:51,840
 We'll put it into a glass globe in a vacuum.

277
00:21:52,660 --> 00:21:53,230
 You'll see.

278
00:21:53,230 --> 00:21:57,480
 One day, everybody will use this illumination.

279
00:21:57,840 --> 00:21:59,220
 Clean and it's bright.

280
00:22:01,650 --> 00:22:05,100
 Full steam ahead.

281
00:22:05,660 --> 00:22:08,350
 To light up a whole city, we'll need a more powerful dynamo.

282
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:09,930
 I'd better work on it.

283
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:15,270
 Faraday demonstrated that movement could be used to produce electricity, and electricity produced movement.

284
00:22:15,530 --> 00:22:17,730
 And so, I can make a train run on electricity.

285
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:20,070
 With my giant dynamo, I can light whole sectors.

286
00:22:20,070 --> 00:22:20,770
 It's incredible.

287
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:26,330
 For the first time in history, a whole sector of a city would have electric lighting.

288
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:30,720
 Yeah, but hold on. That's not all. You'll see. There's also...

289
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:31,890
 Yes, hello?

290
00:22:32,370 --> 00:22:34,420
 No, ma'am. I am not the butcher on the corner.

291
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:37,000
 No, I don't have spare ribs. In fact, I'm fat.

292
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,910
 Did Edison also invent the telephone, too?

293
00:22:40,150 --> 00:22:43,200
 You know, Alexander Graham Bell and Alicia Gray invented that.

294
00:22:43,850 --> 00:22:47,270
 But he added things, and in the end, his system was adopted.

295
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:49,280
 But just wait, there's more yet.

296
00:22:50,630 --> 00:22:53,310
 Madam Mary Rose, I perfected my phonograph.

297
00:22:53,750 --> 00:22:56,810
 I believe that now it is equal to the beauty of your voice.

298
00:22:58,080 --> 00:22:59,410
 To Edison, what an...

299
00:22:59,410 --> 00:23:02,140
 No crank for me to turn?

300
00:23:02,670 --> 00:23:06,050
 No, a spring motor drives the phonograph all by itself now.

301
00:23:06,860 --> 00:23:08,450
 Do I sing?

302
00:23:09,210 --> 00:23:10,190
 If you please.

303
00:23:13,070 --> 00:23:18,120
 How are we doing, Dixon?

304
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:19,600
 It works. Look at this.

305
00:23:22,910 --> 00:23:27,080
 Of course, there's still work to do.

306
00:23:28,690 --> 00:23:32,490
 You see, the platform turns, and so it always faces the sun.

307
00:23:32,860 --> 00:23:33,440
 You see?

308
00:23:33,560 --> 00:23:33,920
 Mm-hmm.

309
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:35,920
 Ready for you, Miss Mary Rose.

310
00:23:39,330 --> 00:23:46,000
 And two Frenchmen a few years later,

311
00:23:46,530 --> 00:23:49,730
 Louis and Auguste Lumiere would perfect the cinema camera,

312
00:23:50,030 --> 00:23:51,670
 but it's Edison who invented it.

313
00:23:52,180 --> 00:23:54,380
 Perhaps Edison wasn't really a great scientist.

314
00:23:54,960 --> 00:23:56,720
 More of an inventor, you might say.

315
00:23:57,080 --> 00:24:00,480
 Literally, he is the one who ushered in the modern era of communications,

316
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:03,640
 and we can thank him for many of the wonderful devices

317
00:24:03,640 --> 00:24:05,480
 that we use in our everyday lives today.

318
00:24:07,860 --> 00:24:13,440
 It's in part thanks to Edison that I'm here with you all today, children, telling his story.

